## Architecture and lifecycle contract

Model the primitive with explicit states and observable transitions:

`Allocating -> Starting_server -> Waiting_ready -> Starting_frontend -> Running`

Terminal paths are `Frontend_exited -> Stopping_server -> Offline` and
`Failed -> Cleaning_up -> Offline`. App-server death while the frontend is
running must terminate/report the unit; frontend exit must always stop the
server. No normal exit path leaves an unattended app-server.

The endpoint is local-only by default and protected by the exact boundary T001
proved. Authentication material is generated per managed unit, passed without
appearing in argv/process listings when the proven design permits, stored only
where required with least privilege, never logged, and deleted on cleanup.
Socket/path permissions are defense in depth, not the authentication proof.

Persist only non-secret identity needed by later tasks: app-server/thread
identity, managed-instance identity, capability/version metadata, lifecycle
state, and endpoint locator if safe. Use atomic writes and define stale-state
recovery. A restart must never silently attach to an endpoint whose ownership
or credential cannot be verified.

## Deliverables and receipt

- A focused internal OCaml module/API for starting, observing, and stopping the
  paired process unit, wired into the build but not exposed as new public CLI.
- Fixture-gated unit/integration tests covering spawn argv/env, readiness,
  state transitions, authentication rejection, crashes, signals, and cleanup.
- A real tmux dogfood receipt under `.collab/research/` containing sanitized
  process/state evidence and return codes; no credential values.
- Module-level documentation stating ownership boundaries for T003, T006, and
  T007 so later agents do not reintroduce public CLI or delivery policy here.

## Acceptance criteria

- The internal API can start the authenticated app-server, wait for verified
  readiness, attach the stock remote TUI, expose its stable thread/server
  identity, and stop the whole unit deterministically.
- Every transition in the lifecycle contract has a fixture test, including
  server-start failure, readiness timeout, frontend-start failure, frontend
  normal exit, frontend signal, server crash, parent signal, and stale state.
- The T001 boundary is enforced. An unrelated same-UID test client without the
  capability cannot connect or invoke passive or active app-server methods.
- No TCP listener is created by default. Any non-local transport support is out
  of scope unless T001 explicitly proved and required it; it must never be an
  implicit fallback.
- Credentials do not appear in logs, status JSON, error text, command-line
  arguments, peer-readable files, or the research receipt. Failure cleanup
  removes endpoints and secrets.
- Frontend exit marks the internal unit offline and stops/reaps app-server and
  helper children; repeated stop/cleanup is idempotent and leaves no zombies.
- Unsupported Codex version/capability fails before opening the frontend with a
  structured diagnostic consumable by T006; existing hook-backed Codex launch
  remains untouched.
- No public command/flag behavior, alias policy, `--yolo`, message injection,
  or `turn/start` is implemented in this task.

## Verification commands

Run the focused tests added by this task plus:

```sh
just build
just check
./scripts/c2c_tmux.py list
```

Use `scripts/c2c_tmux.py` for the documented live launch/exit/crash cases and
record child PIDs before and after to prove that all processes were reaped.

## Blockers

Blocked on P1.M1.E1.T001. If T001 is `NO_GO`, or lacks a positive authenticated
same-UID control boundary, do not implement a weaker launcher: leave this task
blocked and retain Codex hooks as the supported delivery path.
